 for  Apple when the highest court in the  United States decided to go through with  the lawsuit perhaps to an extent Apple's  share price fell on that news  so all of this aside what does it mean  if Apple loses this case potentially it  could mean that Apple may have to offer  partial refunds on all iPhone apps since  the iPhone was introduced in 2007  needless to say this would put a huge  dent in Apple's cash pile Apple has paid  app developers thirty four billion  dollars in 2018 alone so what numbers of  that large refunding 30% of those  hosting costs over the past ten plus  years would be pretty devastating for  the company Apple may also be forced to  allow alternative methods for buying  apps ones where they don't take a cut  this would theoretically make apps  cheaper and more accessible to consumers  if Apple loses then it could set a  precedent for other tech platforms to be  similarly sued and this could have a  knock-on effect across the whole tech  sector for example Microsoft recently  announced a diskless Xbox meaning if  games are only available through their  store then Microsoft may also be accused  of running a monopoly the case even has  risky implications for Amazon and Google  but even if Apple wins the Supreme Court  has removed a massive hurdle in filing  antitrust lawsuits against platforms  this means that there could be more  suits in the future regardless of the  outcome of this case the landscape of  tech may be altered by this lawsuit on  the other side there have been many  voices and politics and in the public  calling for the breakup of large tech  companies in a short space of time a  handful of companies have gained so much  momentum that they are not only worth  billions but also holds so much control  over their markets to a point where this  has never been seen before so on the one  hand potentially this case may make it  easier to break up big tech monopolies  but on the other hand there might be  some unintended consequences it seems to  me that this is really an assault on our  free enterprise system because it's  no-fault monopolies under her proposal  if you reach twenty five billion dollars  in revenues you've got to split up it's  a drastic measure that doesn't comport  with where our antitrust laws are today  could harm innovation and honestly could  end up harming consumers in the long run  so this case is far from over it's  really just begun but the Supreme Court  stance has been noted there will be an  interesting minefield that tech  companies will have to navigate in the  coming years  they have to fight their competitors  while at the same time maintain public  trust and that's a difficult line to  walk politicians and growing public  distrust will undoubtably make this job  more difficult so what do you think does  Apple have a monopoly on the app market  and what do you think about the general  idea of suing companies for getting too  big let me know your thoughts in the  comments section below so an update to  this video as it was being made some  news has broken there could be a  potential antitrust investigation by the  Justice Department and the Federal Trade  Commission against Google Facebook and  Amazon Google is claimed to have a  monopoly in their Play Store and Google  search Amazon may have investigations  into their low prices and Facebook may  have to let go of Instagram and whatsapp  and also have the influence curtailed  needless to say this is going to be a  wild ride  [Music]  so thanks for watching this video this  has been to gogo and you've been  watching cold fusion if you're  interested in topics like this and much  more you should definitely consider  subscribing alright so I'll catch you  again soon for the next video  cheers guys have a good one  [Music]
 

